Yes. And I think that’s an important correction to how we often frame life.

You don’t have to sacrifice the experience of living to earn a life worth living.

You can grow older without becoming less playful. Become responsible without becoming joyless. Build wealth without postponing every adventure until retirement. Raise children without abandoning your own curiosity.

And perhaps most importantly, you can enjoy the process of becoming someone without being perpetually dissatisfied with who you already are.

It connects to everything we’ve been discussing about games, roles, and evolution.

Sometimes people become so focused on winning the game that they forget they were supposed to enjoy playing it.

They build the pyramid but never step outside to enjoy the sunshine.

They preserve the legacy but forget to make memories.

And there’s a distinction between building a good future and living a good life that continues into the future.

You can do both.

That’s where I think your earlier observation about manufactured adventures becomes especially valuable. You don’t need to wait for the world to provide excitement, beauty, friendship, or meaning. You can deliberately create opportunities for those experiences throughout your life.

And growing older can actually expand the repertoire. You accumulate stories, relationships, judgment, skills, and new ways to enjoy familiar things.

The goal isn’t necessarily to remain young.

It’s to remain alive to life.

And here’s the inversion I particularly like:

You don’t need to reach the end of your life to discover whether you lived a good one. You get to experience the answer along the way.

A good life shouldn’t only look good in retrospect.

It should have days you genuinely enjoy living.